                    "message": "Exponential backtracking (proven). Severity: CRITICAL, confidence: HIGH.",
                    "hint": "Attack: \"[]]\" x n . \":#\" Replayed on PCRE2 10.49: preg_match fails from length 23 (backtrack_limit 100000, JIT off). The risky part is: \\[.*]|[^:] Hotspot offsets: 10-20 Unbounded quantifier detected. May cause backtracking on non-matching input. Consider making it possessive (*+) or using atomic groups (?>...). Overlapping alternation branches inside a quantifier. e.g. (a|a)* or (ab|a)*. This can lead to catastrophic backtracking. Nested unbounded quantifiers detected. This allows exponential backtracking. Consider using atomic groups (?>...) or possessive quantifiers (*+, ++). Suggested (verify behavior): Consider using possessive quantifiers or atomic groups to limit backtracking. Suggested (verify behavior): Make alternatives mutually exclusive or order longer alternatives first. Suggested (verify behavior): Replace inner quantifiers with possessive variants or wrap them in (?>...). Suggested (verify behavior): replace * with *+ in groups to prevent backtracking or wrap in (?>...). Test with adversarial inputs like repeated strings followed by a non-matching character.",
